<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Beikthano</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 400 CE (approx.) · capital Beikthano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the oldest Pyu cities in Myanmar, whose name means 'Vishnu city' and whose excavated monastery and burials show early contact with India.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Beikthano grew up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magway-region">Magway region</a> of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar</a> from around the 2nd century BCE, making it among the earliest walled cities of the Pyu. Radiocarbon dates from excavations in the 1950s and 1960s place its main occupation between about 200 BCE and 400 CE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was enclosed by a large, roughly rectangular brick wall, with irrigation works feeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields around it. Excavators found a palace site, a monastery and stupa resembling those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a> in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and many burial urns holding cremated remains, suggesting early Buddhist practice alongside local customs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Beikthano was abandoned or declined around the 4th or 5th century, possibly after a fire, as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-ksetra-kingdom">Sri Ksetra</a> rose as the leading Pyu centre. Local legend links its end to a princess named Panhtwa. It is now one of the three Pyu ancient cities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/beikthano.jpg" alt="Beikthano" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Yarzaryeni · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_Beikthano_Historical_Sites.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beikthano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pyu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vishnu City, Panhtwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=beikthano" aria-label="Beikthano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7253 1437 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Beikthano lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7573 1693L7565 1604"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7565 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7573 1693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7573 1693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=beikthano">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

